4. Hardware and Deployment Strategy

The initial development environment is:

* A 2020 Intel Mac.
* A Raspberry Pi 4.
* Six mobile phones, which will initially act as cameras.
* A local Wi-Fi network.

The Mac will initially be the central processing server.

The Raspberry Pi 4 should act primarily as:

* Camera gateway.
* Stream receiver.
* Lightweight video-processing node.
* Frame buffer.
* Network relay.

It should not be assumed to have sufficient capacity for sophisticated multi-camera AI inference.

The exact Pi configuration (RAM, OS and available hardware) should be detected or requested rather than assumed.

Hardware portability is mandatory

The central application must be independent of the underlying inference hardware.

Initially:

* CPU-based inference on Intel Mac.

Future possibilities:

* Apple Silicon and Metal/MPS.
* Core ML for mobile or Apple-device inference.
* NVIDIA CUDA.
* NVIDIA TensorRT.
* ONNX Runtime.

Introduce an abstraction layer for model execution so that the perception and intelligence modules do not depend directly on a particular inference backend.

Do not assume Apple MPS is available on the Intel Mac.

5. Camera Architecture

The initial camera setup will use six mobile phones.

The intended arrangement is:

* Four cameras covering the four court boundary areas.
* Two additional cameras providing dedicated kitchen-line coverage.

Camera positioning may evolve to provide better overlapping coverage, player visibility and 3D reconstruction.

The camera system must support:

* Camera ID.
* Camera position.
* Camera orientation.
* Resolution.
* Frame rate.
* Timestamp.
* Camera calibration parameters.
* Connection status.
* Stream health.

Initially, mobile phones can stream video to the central server.

Possible technologies:

* RTSP.
* H.264.
* FFmpeg.
* Custom streaming using native iOS AVFoundation or Android CameraX.

Eventually, I may develop custom mobile camera applications.

Important future requirement: Edge intelligence

The system must support two input modes.

Mode A: Raw video.

Camera sends video to the server, which performs all computer vision and AI inference.

Mode B: Edge-generated metadata.

Camera or edge device performs some inference locally and transmits:

* Object coordinates.
* Object classifications.
* Confidence scores.
* Player keypoints.
* Timestamps.
* Frame identifiers.
* Camera identifiers.
* Other observations.

The central server reconstructs the game from these observations.

Design the ingestion interface to support both modes.

Do not make metadata transmission a prerequisite for the initial implementation.

Retain access to original video because it will be important for replay, debugging, model improvement and resolving ambiguous detections.

6. Universal Event Model

Create a common event and observation representation.

Distinguish between:

1. Raw observations: What a camera or detector observed.
2. Reconstructed physical events: What physically happened, such as a ball bounce or paddle contact.
3. Interpreted sport events: What the event means under the sport’s rules.

For example:

A camera detecting a ball is not necessarily evidence that a bounce occurred.

A reconstructed bounce is not automatically an IN or OUT decision.

An interpreted event should retain references to its supporting observations and confidence values.

A common event envelope should contain fields such as:

* Event ID.
* Sport ID.
* Event type.
* Timestamp.
* Participants.
* Objects.
* Spatial coordinates.
* Confidence.
* Supporting observation references.
* Metadata.

Use typed Pydantic models and version the event schemas.

Spatial coordinates must be clearly defined, including the coordinate system, units, origin and reference frame.

7. Pickleball Intelligence Module

The initial sport-specific module should support the following capabilities.

A. Automated line calling

* Detect the ball.
* Track its movement across frames.
* Reconstruct its trajectory.
* Estimate the ground-contact point.
* Compare the contact point against calibrated court boundaries.
* Produce IN, OUT or UNCERTAIN.
* Store confidence.
* Retain the relevant video frames for replay.

Do not use the apparent centre of the ball as a substitute for the physical contact point.

Consider multi-camera triangulation and trajectory estimation.

Line calling must account for the physical ball radius and the rules governing whether a ball touching a line is IN.

The system should abstain when confidence is insufficient rather than making unsupported definitive calls.

B. Player detection and tracking

* Detect all players.
* Maintain player identities throughout rallies.
* Estimate player positions.
* Track movement.
* Estimate court coverage.
* Track movement towards and away from the kitchen.
* Estimate recovery to ready position.

C. Ball and shot tracking

Identify:

* Serves.
* Returns.
* Forehands.
* Backhands.
* Dinks.
* Volleys.
* Third-shot drops.
* Drives.
* Lobs.
* Overheads.

Use a combination of ball trajectory, player pose, paddle movement and temporal context.

Do not assume that a single video frame is sufficient to classify a stroke.

D. Match analytics

Potential statistics:

* Winners.
* Unforced errors.
* Forced errors.
* Aces.
* Service faults.
* Net errors.
* Wide and long errors.
* Rally length.
* Dink success rate.
* Third-shot drop success rate.
* Volley success rate.
* Return depth.
* Shot distribution.
* Player positioning.

Distinguish objectively observable events from subjective classifications.

For example, a ball hitting the net can be detected objectively, but deciding whether it was an unforced error requires contextual analysis.

Support uncertain classifications and manual corrections.

E. Technique analysis

Use human pose estimation, paddle detection and temporal movement analysis.

Analyse:

* Forehand technique.
* Backhand technique.
* Serve technique.
* Dinking technique.
* Volley technique.
* Ready position.
* Recovery position.
* Footwork.
* Body rotation.
* Shoulder rotation.
* Elbow positioning.
* Contact height.
* Paddle orientation.
* Follow-through.

Do not generate arbitrary technique scores.

Start with measurable biomechanical features and establish validated scoring criteria before generating recommendations.

Technique assessments should retain supporting video clips and explain their reasoning.

